<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alulim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alulim is the first king named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>, which states that after kingship descended from heaven it was at Eridu, where Alulim reigned for 28,800 years. He is a legendary figure belonging to the list's mythical era before the flood. His successor at Eridu is given as Alalngar, after which kingship passed to other cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alulim.jpg" alt="Alulim" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lista_Reale_Sumerica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Alulim was a mythological Mesopotamian ruler, regarded as the first king ever to rule. Assyriologists consider him a fictional figure, and he is absent from Early Dynastic sources. Mentions of him are largely limited to lists of mythical ancient rulers, which place his reign before the great flood. All known sources listing primordial kings consistently place him in Eridu, a city of particular symbolic importance, unless they name no city.</p><p>His name was written A2-lu-lim or A-lu-lim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> and can be translated from Sumerian as either horn of the red deer or seed of the red deer, depending on the first sign. Jeremiah Peterson suggests that it may reflect the Mesopotamian belief that humans at the dawn of history behaved like animals. Variant forms include Alulu and Ayalu, the latter probably shaped by the word ayyalu, deer or stag. The name was never used as an ordinary given name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>, Alulim held the kingship for 28,800 years before Alalngar succeeded him and reigned for 36,000 years. The Ballad of Early Rulers gives him 36,000 years, and other texts give figures such as 36,200 years (tablet BM 40565) and 67,200 years (tablet WB 62), according to Irving Finkel. The King List is assumed not to reflect historical reality and cannot be used to reconstruct early Mesopotamian chronology. His long reign became proverbial: the astrologer Ašarēdu wrote to a Babylonian king wishing him the years of Alulu.</p><p>A tablet of the Eridu Genesis from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, published in 2018 and dating to the Old Babylonian period, describes the gods An, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a> choosing Alulim from among vast and many people to be shepherd of the entire population. It has been read as an explanation of how kingship began, with the first ruler chosen to guide still animal-like humans. Peterson notes that its grammar might not mark Alulim as a human at all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the Ballad of Early Rulers Alulim stands among ancient figures such as Etana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra">Ziusudra</a>, Humbaba and Enkidu. Neo-Babylonian incantations against field pests call him a king from before the deluge and the creator of insects, able to expel them with a wand. In late tradition the sage Adapa became his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, who gave him wisdom on behalf of the god Ea.</p><p>The Greek form of his name, Aloros, appears in citations of Berossus preserved by Eusebius and Syncellus, where he reigns 36,000 years and is followed by Alaparos. In the Tabaqat-i Nasiri, written in 1259–1260, Minhaj-i Siraj Juzjani gives a derivative, Aylūrūs, as the first king of the people opposed to giants and equates him with Gayūmart.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alulim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sumerian King List opens with the words that kingship first descended from heaven at Eridu, naming the legendary kings <b>Alulim</b> and Alalngar.</blockquote><a href="/state/eridu">Eridu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eridu, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in Eridu, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.83,46" aria-label="Eridu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alulim"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3982 4867h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3982 4867h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.83,46">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
